VALUES ('cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 'secplus-practice-9', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 9', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['Threat Actors', 'Fileless Malware', 'Privilege Escalation', 'Deepfakes', 'IaC Security', 'eDiscovery', 'Bug Bounty', 'FedRAMP', 'SOC 2']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 9, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('7c6a8213-17b2-45de-9488-bc66b2db5176', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 1, 'A company assesses the impact of a hypothetical data breach across several distinct dimensions: the direct cost of remediation and regulatory fines, the loss of customer trust and negative media coverage, the disruption to normal business operations while systems are being restored, and the potential for lawsuits or regulatory enforcement action. What are these distinct dimensions of impact called, and why does considering all of them matter?');
VALUES ('7c6a8213-17b2-45de-9488-bc66b2db5176', 'dfbdf6fb-7e64-4757-b022-89caf98ab6b9', 'Impact categories provide a genuinely important, multi-dimensional framework for risk assessment specifically because a narrow, single-dimension view — most commonly, evaluating only direct FINANCIAL cost — can badly understate an incident''s true total significance, directly complementing the quantitative ALE calculation (covered in a prior paper, which itself produces a single financial figure) with the broader, non-purely-financial dimensions that figure alone doesn''t capture. Reputational impact (lost customer trust, negative media coverage) can genuinely outlast and outweigh the direct remediation cost, sometimes for years; operational impact (business disruption, lost productivity while systems are down or being restored) is a real, separate cost distinct from the remediation bill itself; and legal/regulatory impact (lawsuits, enforcement actions, and connecting to specific regulations like GDPR, HIPAA, PCI-DSS, and SOX, all covered across prior papers) can impose costs and consequences having little direct relationship to the technical remediation cost at all. A genuinely thorough risk assessment considers all four dimensions together, since focusing on financial cost alone is exactly the kind of narrow view that leads organizations to systematically underinvest in security relative to their TRUE total exposure.');

VALUES ('6172408c-bb2c-4166-bb5b-44fbe4d5e6f9', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 2, 'A security team profiles different categories of adversaries they might realistically face: a nation-state actor with substantial resources and a specific espionage or sabotage objective, a hacktivist motivated by a political or social cause, an organized crime group motivated purely by financial profit, and an unskilled attacker running pre-built attack tools with little understanding of how they actually work. What is this classification called, and why does it matter for defensive planning?');
VALUES ('6172408c-bb2c-4166-bb5b-44fbe4d5e6f9', '20978c6d-e43b-4bd9-a664-554e077d1426', 'Threat actor typing provides a genuinely useful, realistic framework for proportionate defensive planning, distinct from — though a broader category encompassing — the insider threat concept (covered in a prior paper, which specifically describes an actor with EXISTING, legitimate authorized access, a different characteristic axis from this EXTERNAL-actor-type taxonomy based on resources and motivation). A nation-state actor typically brings substantial resources, patience, and sophistication toward specific geopolitical or espionage goals, often representing the highest-tier threat a typical organization might realistically face, though usually only if the organization''s data or function is genuinely strategically relevant. A hacktivist is motivated by ideology and often specifically seeks public visibility or embarrassment for a cause rather than covert financial gain. Organized crime groups operate with genuine financial motivation, sometimes at real commercial scale and sophistication rivaling nation-state actors specifically for profit-driven attacks like ransomware. Script kiddies represent the lowest sophistication tier, using pre-built, publicly-available tools with limited genuine technical understanding, but can still cause real damage particularly against poorly-defended targets. Understanding which of these an organization realistically faces genuinely should inform proportionate, realistic defensive prioritization — treating every threat as equally likely and equally sophisticated wastes limited security resources on the wrong priorities.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('c27ce1a1-911f-4286-b221-3097dd750ddd', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 3, 'An attacker compromises a system and, rather than dropping a traditional malicious executable file to disk, instead uses legitimate, already-installed system administration tools (like PowerShell) to carry out malicious actions entirely in memory, leaving little to no trace on the disk itself for traditional file-based antivirus scanning to detect. What is this specific malware/attack technique called?');
VALUES ('c27ce1a1-911f-4286-b221-3097dd750ddd', 'd5b389f1-070b-4649-a679-87972cce7e94', 'Fileless malware is specifically distinguished by AVOIDING the traditional "drop a malicious executable to disk" pattern that most signature-based antivirus (covered generally in a prior paper) is fundamentally built to catch — by instead abusing already-trusted, pre-installed administrative tools and scripting engines (PowerShell being a particularly common example, given its deep legitimate system access) to carry out malicious actions primarily or entirely in memory, fileless techniques leave meaningfully less for traditional file-scanning to actually examine, since the "malware" is really just legitimate tools being MISUSED rather than a distinctly identifiable malicious file existing on disk at all. This is exactly why EDR (covered generally in a prior paper, which watches BEHAVIOR rather than relying purely on file signatures) is a genuinely more effective detection approach against this specific technique — a behavioral monitor can flag PowerShell being used in an anomalous, suspicious WAY, even though PowerShell itself is completely legitimate and wouldn''t itself ever be flagged by a file-signature-based scan. This is genuinely different from a rootkit (covered in a prior paper, defined by OS-level CONCEALMENT of an existing compromise, a different characteristic from fileless malware''s specific avoidance of ever dropping a file at all) and a logic bomb (covered in a prior paper, defined by delayed, condition-based TRIGGERING, an unrelated characteristic) — this is a real, well-documented, and increasingly common technique specifically because it genuinely evades a meaningful category of traditional detection.');

VALUES ('8b09a23b-aa4c-4fd9-a281-26c9f381d2b8', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 4, 'An attacker within physical proximity of a target''s Bluetooth-enabled device exploits a vulnerability to access and copy the device''s stored contact list and messages without the device owner''s knowledge or authorization — distinct from simply sending the victim an unsolicited, unwanted message over Bluetooth. What is this specific, more severe Bluetooth attack called?');
VALUES ('8b09a23b-aa4c-4fd9-a281-26c9f381d2b8', '1af24112-af20-4c50-9075-7b8088f02097', 'Bluesnarfing is specifically distinguished from bluejacking by the SEVERITY and NATURE of what actually happens — bluejacking is essentially a nuisance-level attack, sending an unsolicited, unwanted message to a nearby Bluetooth-discoverable device (annoying, but not itself a confidentiality breach of any real stored data), while bluesnarfing represents an actual, genuine theft of the device''s real stored data — contacts, messages, potentially other files — exploiting a Bluetooth protocol or implementation vulnerability to access that data without the device owner''s knowledge or authorization at all. This is a genuinely important, directly testable severity distinction between two similarly-named Bluetooth attack terms that are frequently confused given how similar they sound — a real, meaningful difference between "someone sent me an annoying message" and "someone silently stole my contact list." This connects to the broader wireless security theme covered elsewhere (evil twin, covered in a prior paper, for Wi-Fi impersonation; WPA2/WPA3, covered in a prior paper, for Wi-Fi encryption) extended specifically to the Bluetooth protocol — this is a real, well-documented Bluetooth vulnerability class, not a technical impossibility requiring the victim''s active participation.');

VALUES ('c6dda936-b77d-44e3-868c-380dcdf8906b', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 5, 'An attacker who has compromised a low-privileged standard user account on a system exploits a separate vulnerability to elevate that account''s access to full administrator/root level on the SAME system. A DIFFERENT attacker, having compromised one standard user account, instead accesses a DIFFERENT user''s account at the SAME privilege level (not elevating to a higher level at all, but moving to a different, equally-privileged account). What are these two distinct privilege-escalation patterns called?');
VALUES ('c6dda936-b77d-44e3-868c-380dcdf8906b', '35d58512-8dc5-4b9c-b70f-d291867dabb6', 'Vertical and horizontal privilege escalation are genuinely distinguished by the DIRECTION of the escalation — vertical specifically means moving UP the privilege hierarchy (standard user to administrator, or administrator to root/system-level access), a genuine increase in the attacker''s overall level of control and capability on the compromised system, directly connecting to why least privilege (covered generally in a prior paper) matters as a mitigation — a compromised account with genuinely minimal privileges to begin with has meaningfully less to escalate FROM. Horizontal escalation instead means moving SIDEWAYS — accessing a DIFFERENT account or resource at the SAME privilege tier, without actually gaining any higher-level access at all — for example, a compromised standard user account being used to access a DIFFERENT standard user''s files or data, expanding what the attacker can reach without ever obtaining administrator rights. Both represent genuine attacker goals AFTER an initial compromise, and separation of duties and least privilege (both covered generally in prior papers) specifically help limit the practical impact of either escalation pattern by constraining what any single compromised account can actually reach or do, even before any escalation attempt succeeds. This is a real, well-documented, and directly testable distinction — a compromised account''s initial access level is emphatically NOT always a fixed, permanent ceiling, which is precisely the vulnerability this pair of concepts describes exploiting.');

VALUES ('f7b895d4-9edd-4f50-9441-00b178593dc4', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 6, 'An attacker creates a highly realistic, AI-generated synthetic video of a company''s CEO appearing to give urgent, legitimate-sounding verbal instructions to the finance team to wire a large sum of money to a specific account — the video and voice are convincing enough that finance staff genuinely believe they''re watching and hearing the real CEO. What is this AI-generated synthetic media technique called, and how does it relate to BEC?');
VALUES ('f7b895d4-9edd-4f50-9441-00b178593dc4', '1f6c6c35-68eb-4877-9c1f-7046003ecbc6', 'Deepfakes represent a genuinely real, increasingly capable evolution of the social engineering threat landscape, directly relevant to and extending BEC (covered in a prior paper, which classically relies on a spoofed or lookalike EMAIL address impersonating a trusted party) — rather than a text-based email a target might scrutinize for subtle inconsistencies, a sufficiently convincing deepfake video or audio genuinely mimicking a trusted executive''s actual appearance and voice adds a dramatically more psychologically persuasive, harder-to-doubt layer to the exact same underlying manipulation goal: getting a target to take an urgent financial or data-disclosure action based on apparent authority. This is genuinely distinct from pretexting (covered in a prior paper, which relies on a FABRICATED SCENARIO/STORY as its core mechanism, not synthetic media specifically) and baiting (covered in a prior paper, which relies on an enticing LURE, an entirely different mechanism) — deepfake technology is a real, demonstrated, and increasingly accessible capability (not a hypothetical or non-existent one), which is exactly why organizations are increasingly adopting specific verification procedures (like an independently-initiated callback to a known number, rather than trusting a video/audio call alone) as a direct mitigation against exactly this evolving threat.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('76130181-f032-447c-90a4-3cb53a0e58aa', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 7, 'A company evaluates several ways to deploy its infrastructure: entirely within a public cloud provider''s shared infrastructure, entirely within its own privately-owned and operated data center, a combination of both used together deliberately, and infrastructure spread across MULTIPLE different public cloud providers simultaneously. What are these distinct deployment models called?');
VALUES ('76130181-f032-447c-90a4-3cb53a0e58aa', 'c69376f9-cd10-4ae6-a68d-10a835aa8605', 'These four deployment models directly extend the cloud shared responsibility model concept (covered generally in a prior paper, which focused specifically on public-versus-private security responsibility DIVISION) with the broader classification of the deployment models themselves. Public cloud means the shared, multi-tenant infrastructure a provider makes available to many customers, with the shared-responsibility division covered previously applying directly. Private cloud means an organization runs its OWN dedicated infrastructure with no external multi-tenancy at all — as covered previously, this generally means the organization itself bears MORE total responsibility across every layer, without a provider offloading any of it. Hybrid cloud deliberately combines both, typically routing different specific workloads to whichever environment best fits their particular requirements (a workload with strict data-residency needs staying on private infrastructure, while a more elastic, bursty workload runs in public cloud). Multi-cloud specifically means deliberately spreading infrastructure across MULTIPLE different public cloud PROVIDERS simultaneously — a genuinely distinct strategy from hybrid cloud''s public-plus-private combination — often specifically to avoid single-vendor lock-in risk or to leverage different providers'' particular strengths for different workloads. This is a real, standard, and genuinely important architectural classification, with organizations genuinely choosing among all four models based on real, practical trade-offs, not an arbitrary or non-existent distinction.');

VALUES ('b4ff38a5-1854-4474-afcf-bf9a54955a9f', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 8, 'A company defines its entire cloud infrastructure — networks, servers, security groups — as version-controlled CODE (using a tool like Terraform or CloudFormation) rather than manually clicking through a cloud console to configure resources one at a time. A security team wants to understand what NEW security considerations this Infrastructure as Code (IaC) approach specifically introduces, beyond the underlying infrastructure''s own security requirements.');
VALUES ('b4ff38a5-1854-4474-afcf-bf9a54955a9f', '3be91704-dd45-49df-ad66-aae6da80d02d', 'IaC genuinely introduces real, distinct security considerations specifically because the infrastructure DEFINITION itself becomes CODE with its own real security implications, beyond simply securing whatever infrastructure that code eventually produces — directly extending SAST (covered generally in a prior paper, for scanning APPLICATION source code) to IaC-specific scanning tools that check infrastructure templates for security misconfigurations (an overly permissive security group rule, a publicly-exposed resource that shouldn''t be) BEFORE that misconfiguration is ever actually deployed, catching problems earlier and more cheaply than discovering them after live deployment. The version control system holding that IaC code becomes a genuinely high-value target in its own right — since a successful compromise there could let an attacker inject a malicious infrastructure change (opening an unauthorized network path, weakening a security control) that then gets AUTOMATICALLY deployed through the normal IaC pipeline, a real and meaningfully different attack surface from simply attacking the deployed infrastructure directly. And IaC templates frequently need to reference genuinely sensitive values (database credentials, API keys) — hardcoding those directly into version-controlled code is a real, well-documented anti-pattern (since version history often persists that sensitive value even after later removal, and repository access effectively becomes credential access), which is exactly why external secret storage (Secrets Manager-style patterns, covered generally elsewhere) referenced dynamically at deployment time, rather than embedded directly in the code, is the standard, recommended approach.');

VALUES ('9dc81d6f-ac12-460f-a09f-0e8c89a210ef', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 9, 'A company protecting a physical data center deploys a layered set of physical security controls: CCTV cameras providing continuous visual monitoring and a recorded evidentiary trail, uniformed security guards performing active patrols and visitor verification, and concrete bollards positioned around the building''s perimeter specifically to prevent a vehicle from being deliberately driven into the structure. What is the general security principle these three DIFFERENT types of physical controls collectively illustrate?');
VALUES ('9dc81d6f-ac12-460f-a09f-0e8c89a210ef', '23b8bf52-e58b-4bd4-b3db-3c132b82069c', 'This directly extends defense-in-depth (covered generally in a prior paper, in the context of network/logical controls like firewalls and endpoint protection working together) into the PHYSICAL security domain specifically — the three example controls here genuinely occupy different roles within the control-TYPE taxonomy covered generally in a prior paper: CCTV is primarily a DETECTIVE control (recording what happened, providing evidence and after-the-fact visibility, though its visible presence also carries some deterrent value), security guards combine active DETERRENT presence with genuine, human-judgment-based DETECTIVE and response capability that a passive camera alone fundamentally cannot provide (a guard can actively investigate something suspicious and respond in real time, where a camera can only record it), and bollards are a purely PREVENTIVE physical control, specifically and directly blocking a particular attack vector (vehicle-ramming) outright before it could ever succeed. Layering these genuinely different control types together — rather than relying on any single one alone — directly mirrors why defense-in-depth matters in the logical/network domain: no single control type, physical or logical, realistically provides complete protection against every plausible threat on its own, and combining genuinely different, complementary control types closes gaps that any one type alone would leave open. A mantrap (covered generally in a prior paper) and a Faraday cage (covered generally in a prior paper) are both specific, individual PHYSICAL CONTROLS themselves, not the broader organizing principle this question asks about.');

VALUES ('0bfe161b-54d0-4b3b-96fe-ca4a39595db8', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 10, 'A network architect describes traffic flowing BETWEEN different servers/services WITHIN the same data center or VPC (e.g., a web server calling an internal API service) as one category, and traffic flowing IN or OUT of that data center/VPC boundary to/from external clients or the internet as a different category. What are these two traffic-direction categories called, and why does distinguishing them matter for network segmentation strategy?');
VALUES ('0bfe161b-54d0-4b3b-96fe-ca4a39595db8', 'fb556b73-21ce-4451-bd85-02f0e92243fd', 'This East-West/North-South distinction directly explains WHY micro-segmentation (covered generally in a prior paper) matters as a genuinely distinct control from perimeter-focused defenses like a DMZ (covered generally in a prior paper) — traditional network security architecture historically concentrated heavily on the North-South boundary (the perimeter separating trusted internal infrastructure from the untrusted external internet), operating on an implicit assumption that once traffic was inside that perimeter, it could largely be trusted to move relatively freely between internal systems. This assumption creates a genuine, real vulnerability: an attacker who successfully breaches the North-South perimeter just once can then often move relatively unrestricted EAST-WEST between internal systems, directly enabling exactly the kind of lateral movement that turns a single initial compromise into a much broader, more damaging breach. Micro-segmentation directly addresses this by applying meaningful access controls to East-West traffic ITSELF, not just at the North-South perimeter — treating internal network segments with the same "don''t automatically trust" scrutiny previously reserved mainly for the external boundary, directly connecting to and operationalizing the Zero Trust principle (covered generally in a prior paper, "never trust, always verify") specifically at the internal-network-traffic level. This is a real, standard, and genuinely important distinction in modern network security architecture, not an arbitrary or meaningless traffic-direction label.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('df0a582d-cc56-47b3-bde4-a81e18120666', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 11, 'A company facing active litigation must identify, preserve, collect, and eventually produce all potentially RELEVANT electronic information (emails, documents, chat logs) across its systems for the opposing party''s legal review — a broader, more comprehensive process than simply placing a legal hold (covered generally in a prior paper) to pause routine deletion. What is this broader, structured process called?');
VALUES ('df0a582d-cc56-47b3-bde4-a81e18120666', '9d8890fe-b9f6-4b6a-9d8d-e257bca0df23', 'eDiscovery is specifically the BROADER, end-to-end legal process, genuinely encompassing more than just the preservation step a legal hold (covered generally in a prior paper) specifically addresses — a legal hold pauses routine deletion so potentially relevant data survives long enough to actually be found and used, but eDiscovery as a complete process also includes actually IDENTIFYING where relevant data might exist across potentially many different systems, COLLECTING it in a way that''s legally defensible (connecting to chain of custody and forensic imaging concepts, covered generally in prior papers, since evidence collected in a forensically unsound way can be challenged), REVIEWING it (often for both relevance and legal privilege before disclosure), and ultimately PRODUCING the relevant material to the opposing party or a court. This is a genuinely important, directly testable distinction — legal hold is one specific STEP within the larger eDiscovery process, not a synonym for the whole thing, and a write blocker (covered generally in a prior paper) is a specific forensic TOOL used during the collection step, not the overarching legal process itself. eDiscovery is a real, standard, and legally significant structured process organizations facing litigation genuinely must follow, not an ad hoc or informal undertaking.');

VALUES ('b80d8b59-a1a9-4924-a6e1-78cf99d011e4', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 12, 'A company runs two different vulnerability scanning approaches: one installs a small piece of software directly on each target system that continuously reports findings back to a central console, while the other scans targets remotely over the network with no software installed on the targets themselves. What are these two distinct scanning approaches called, and what is the key trade-off between them?');
VALUES ('b80d8b59-a1a9-4924-a6e1-78cf99d011e4', '4e7f1b09-aff6-457d-8eeb-ea8755b269f8', 'This refines the general vulnerability scanning concept (covered generally in prior papers, including the credentialed-vs-non-credentialed distinction) with a genuinely important, directly testable DEPLOYMENT-MODEL distinction: agent-based scanning installs a small, persistent piece of software directly on each individual target, which then continuously monitors and reports findings back to a central console — genuinely well suited to systems that move frequently between different networks (a traveling employee''s laptop, which might not always be reachable for a remote scan) or systems sitting behind restrictive network segmentation that would otherwise block a remote scanner from ever reaching them at all, at the real, ongoing cost of needing to deploy, maintain, and keep that agent software itself patched and updated across an entire fleet — a genuine, non-trivial management burden of its own. Agentless scanning instead reaches out and scans targets remotely over the network with nothing installed on the target itself, which is genuinely simpler to deploy at scale with zero per-target agent management overhead, but is correspondingly limited by whatever network reachability actually exists between the scanner and the target (a target behind a restrictive firewall segment the scanner can''t reach simply won''t get scanned), and by whether sufficient access/credentials are available to produce genuinely accurate results, directly connecting to the credentialed-scan concept covered generally in a prior paper. This is a real, genuinely important operational trade-off organizations actively weigh when designing their vulnerability management program, not an arbitrary or non-existent distinction.');

VALUES ('f04af808-a73d-4f0f-839f-9f3758bd9f5e', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 13, 'After a significant security incident is fully resolved, the team holds the lessons-learned review (covered generally in a prior paper) — but specifically wants to use a structured analytical TECHNIQUE during that review to dig past the obvious, surface-level trigger and identify the actual, underlying, systemic cause the incident ultimately stemmed from, rather than stopping at a superficial explanation. What is this kind of structured, underlying-cause-focused analytical technique called?');
VALUES ('f04af808-a73d-4f0f-839f-9f3758bd9f5e', '5c2e65b7-68d1-4a3e-952c-a42afafa9130', 'Root cause analysis is specifically the ANALYTICAL TECHNIQUE applied to dig past a superficial, immediate trigger and reach the genuine, underlying, systemic cause an incident actually stemmed from — a genuinely important, directly testable distinction from the lessons-learned review (covered generally in a prior paper) itself, which is the broader MEETING/PROCESS structure within which a technique like root cause analysis is commonly and specifically applied as one of its structured analytical tools, not a synonym for the review meeting as a whole. The "5 Whys" technique specifically works by repeatedly asking "why" to each successive answer — "why did the breach happen" leads to an immediate technical answer, but asking "why" again about THAT answer, and again, and again, typically surfaces a genuinely deeper, more systemic cause (an inadequate patch management PROCESS, rather than simply "a specific unpatched server") that the immediate, surface-level trigger alone wouldn''t reveal. A fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram is an alternative structured technique organizing potential contributing factors into categories to visually map out possible root causes. This distinction matters practically because addressing only the immediate, surface-level trigger without identifying the genuine underlying systemic cause often means the SAME type of incident recurs later from the same unaddressed root cause — root cause analysis specifically exists to prevent exactly that recurrence, a real and meaningful value distinct from merely patching the immediate symptom.');

VALUES ('631d1ca4-e690-4e58-9e81-fe8494d5576f', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 14, 'A company deploys Data Loss Prevention (DLP, covered generally in a prior paper) in three distinct ways: monitoring traffic AT THE NETWORK LEVEL as data moves across the corporate network, monitoring activity DIRECTLY ON individual employee laptops/endpoints (catching a local file copy to USB, for example), and monitoring data stored within cloud SaaS applications the company uses. What are these three distinct DLP deployment types called, and why might an organization deploy more than one together?');
VALUES ('631d1ca4-e690-4e58-9e81-fe8494d5576f', '93d3ed66-6bc1-47a6-be3d-524ce91d87ff', 'This refines the general DLP concept (covered generally in a prior paper) with the genuinely important, directly testable DEPLOYMENT-LOCATION distinction between three real, complementary DLP types, each with a genuinely different real-world BLIND SPOT the others specifically cover: network DLP inspects data actually crossing the network (email attachments, web uploads, file transfers) but is fundamentally blind to activity that never touches the network at all, like directly copying a file to a locally-attached USB drive. Endpoint DLP closes exactly that gap by running an agent directly on the device itself, able to observe and control genuinely local activity — directly echoing the same agent-based-monitoring trade-off (real visibility gained, at the cost of agent deployment/management overhead) covered earlier this paper for vulnerability scanning. Cloud DLP specifically addresses data living within cloud SaaS applications themselves — connecting directly to the CASB concept covered generally in a prior paper, since a CASB is frequently the actual platform ENFORCING cloud-specific DLP policy — a genuinely different data location from either network-in-transit or endpoint-local data. Deploying multiple DLP types together provides meaningfully more complete coverage precisely because each individual type''s real blind spot is a location the OTHER types are specifically positioned to cover, making a multi-layered DLP deployment a real, standard, and genuinely more effective practice than relying on any single DLP type alone.');

VALUES ('42c04a2d-027c-4d84-ac03-54ed3565932e', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 15, 'A company''s Business Continuity Plan addresses not just IT SYSTEM recovery, but also specifically plans for the scenario where a key LEADER or executive becomes genuinely unavailable during an actual disaster (incapacitated, unreachable, or otherwise unable to fulfill their role) — pre-designating who specifically assumes that person''s critical decision-making authority and responsibilities in that exact scenario. What is this specific planning practice called?');
VALUES ('42c04a2d-027c-4d84-ac03-54ed3565932e', '15b46f10-530d-4e36-843f-11769708eaf1', 'COOP succession planning specifically addresses a genuinely distinct continuity dimension from the IT-systems-and-facility-focused BCP/DR concepts covered generally in prior papers (including hot/warm/cold alternate sites) — even a technically flawless IT recovery plan and a fully-ready alternate site provide little practical value if there''s genuine confusion or a real vacuum in who actually holds critical DECISION-MAKING authority during an active crisis, precisely the kind of high-pressure, time-sensitive situation where clear, pre-established leadership succession matters most. COOP succession planning specifically pre-designates, in a clear, unambiguous order (a "line of succession"), exactly who assumes a key leader''s critical responsibilities and decision-making authority if that person becomes genuinely unavailable — incapacitated, unreachable, or otherwise unable to fulfill their role — during an actual disaster scenario, ensuring the organization''s ability to make critical decisions and take necessary action continues even if a specific key individual cannot personally be involved. This is a real, genuinely important, and complementary component of comprehensive continuity planning, distinct from a hot site (covered generally in a prior paper, an IT/facility recovery concept) and a CSIRT (covered generally in a prior paper, a team ACTIVATED for a specific incident, not an ongoing leadership-succession plan) — leadership and personnel continuity genuinely IS a real, standard, and directly addressed component of mature business continuity planning, not an afterthought.');

VALUES ('91349efc-866e-4d7e-863f-156c60dece83', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 16, 'A software company establishes a formal program specifically inviting independent, external security researchers to search for and report vulnerabilities in the company''s own products, offering a defined process for how to responsibly report a finding and, in some cases, a financial reward proportional to the severity of a genuine, confirmed vulnerability discovered. What is this kind of formal, external-researcher-facing program called?');
VALUES ('91349efc-866e-4d7e-863f-156c60dece83', '4f53091b-8264-41ff-be35-d7d6bb93287c', 'A vulnerability disclosure program (often paired with a bug bounty component) is specifically distinguished from a traditional, scoped penetration test (covered generally in a prior paper, along with its own formal Rules of Engagement, also covered in a prior paper) by being a genuinely OPEN invitation to the broader independent security research community generally, rather than a single, specifically-authorized firm engaged for one defined, time-bound engagement — anyone in the eligible research community can participate on an ongoing basis, rather than the tightly scoped, pre-authorized, time-limited relationship a traditional pentest represents. This addresses a genuinely real, practical problem: independent researchers regularly DO discover real vulnerabilities in products they don''t work for, and without a clear, safe, sanctioned reporting channel and process, a researcher who finds something genuine faces an awkward choice — staying silent (meaning the company never learns about a real, exploitable issue at all), or disclosing it publicly with no advance coordination (which can leave the company with zero opportunity to actually fix the issue before it becomes public and potentially actively exploited). A formal VDP, with a bug bounty specifically offering a genuine financial incentive proportional to severity, gives researchers a real, positive reason to report responsibly through the sanctioned channel rather than defaulting to either alternative — this is a real, standard, and increasingly common industry practice specifically because it provides real, genuine security value that traditional, narrowly-scoped internal testing alone often misses.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('c7ae9fef-9b29-44cb-8d01-70679252cccf', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 17, 'A technology vendor wants to sell its cloud product to US federal government agencies, and discovers this requires obtaining a specific government-mandated security authorization — distinct from any of the private-sector-focused compliance frameworks or regulations covered elsewhere (HIPAA, PCI-DSS, GDPR, SOX) — specifically standardizing security assessment and authorization for cloud services used BY the US federal government. What is this specific government authorization program called?');
VALUES ('c7ae9fef-9b29-44cb-8d01-70679252cccf', 'e2062ed5-b679-4295-b96d-680783e4240f', 'FedRAMP is specifically distinguished by its SCOPE and AUTHORITY — a US federal GOVERNMENT program (not an industry standard like PCI-DSS, covered generally in a prior paper, and not a general federal LAW like SOX, covered generally in a prior paper, which instead governs publicly traded companies'' financial reporting broadly) specifically standardizing how CLOUD services are security-assessed and authorized for actual use BY federal agencies, addressing the government''s own, genuinely distinct procurement and risk-tolerance requirements when adopting cloud technology, requirements meaningfully different from what any private-sector-focused framework addresses. FISMA is the broader federal law governing information security across federal agencies generally, with FedRAMP specifically implementing and standardizing FISMA''s requirements for the cloud-services procurement context in particular — a real, meaningful two-tier relationship (broad law, specific cloud-focused implementation program) rather than two interchangeable names for the same thing. This is genuinely distinct in both scope and authority from every other regulation covered elsewhere across this project''s Security+ content (HIPAA for health information, GDPR for EU personal data broadly, PCI-DSS for payment card data, SOX for financial reporting integrity) — a real, significant, and directly relevant authorization requirement for any vendor genuinely targeting the US federal government as a customer, not an optional or non-existent formality.');

VALUES ('c515889c-d481-4c03-baa5-04868bce276e', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 18, 'A company evaluating a SaaS vendor requests a SOC 2 report as part of their vendor risk assessment (covered generally in a prior paper). The vendor offers a choice between a "Type I" report and a "Type II" report. What is the key difference between these two SOC 2 report types, and which generally provides stronger assurance?');
VALUES ('c515889c-d481-4c03-baa5-04868bce276e', '72da9202-0d47-43b5-94bd-36ad7ff067c4', 'This refines the general third-party audit and vendor risk management concepts (both covered generally in prior papers) with a genuinely important, directly testable nuance specific to SOC 2 reports — a Type I report is a narrower, POINT-IN-TIME assessment, confirming that a vendor''s described security controls are appropriately DESIGNED as of one specific date, but says nothing about whether those controls have actually operated effectively, consistently, and as intended over any meaningful stretch of real time. A Type II report goes meaningfully further, assessing whether those SAME controls genuinely operated effectively over an extended OBSERVATION PERIOD (commonly six to twelve months) — directly verifying sustained, real-world operational effectiveness, not merely a well-designed control existing on paper at a single snapshot moment. This is exactly why Type II is generally considered to provide meaningfully STRONGER assurance for vendor risk assessment purposes — a control that''s well-designed but poorly or inconsistently OPERATED in actual practice provides materially less real protection than the paper design alone might suggest, a gap only a Type II report''s extended observation period can actually verify. Understanding this Type I versus Type II distinction directly and practically matters when a company is genuinely evaluating which specific report type to request or accept as sufficient evidence during vendor due diligence.');

VALUES ('1b2363bf-dc01-4c57-b9ad-3f3d73d69328', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 19, 'A company''s employees have started using public generative AI chatbot tools for work tasks, including occasionally pasting genuinely sensitive internal information (unreleased product plans, customer data) into the chatbot to get help drafting or analyzing it — without realizing that data entered into a public AI tool may be retained, logged, or potentially used by the AI provider in ways the company never explicitly authorized or reviewed. What governance response specifically addresses this?');
VALUES ('1b2363bf-dc01-4c57-b9ad-3f3d73d69328', '6f6333c2-0383-4416-bfa8-3ca8e91325fd', 'A generative AI acceptable use policy directly extends the general AUP concept (covered generally in a prior paper, for acceptable IT usage broadly) with a genuinely modern, increasingly common, and real governance gap this scenario specifically illustrates — employees using a genuinely new, powerful class of external tool with real, non-obvious data-handling implications that most employees have no particular reason to already understand or think carefully about on their own. A well-constructed policy directly connects to and leverages the organization''s existing data classification scheme (covered generally in a prior paper) as its natural foundation — clearly stating, for instance, that Public and Internal-tier data might be acceptable to use with an approved AI tool, while Confidential and Restricted-tier data (connecting to the same classification concept) never should be, giving employees a clear, concrete, and already-familiar framework rather than a vague, generic "be careful" instruction. The policy should also specifically address which AI tools (if any) are actually organizationally approved, since different tools carry genuinely different data-retention and data-usage terms, and should ensure employees genuinely understand that data entered into many public AI tools may be logged, retained, or potentially used in ways the company never explicitly reviewed or approved — a real, legitimate, and non-hypothetical data-exposure risk this policy specifically exists to proactively address before an actual sensitive-data leak occurs, not merely after.');

VALUES ('e08922b0-f9ce-4ac9-b7d8-61b543ab825d', 'cde3aa69-4bbc-49cd-9577-f5f79381db57', 20, 'A company wants to test its Business Continuity/Disaster Recovery plan using progressively more rigorous methods: first, simply reviewing the written plan document as a group discussion (covered generally as a tabletop exercise in a prior paper); then, actually walking through the specific step-by-step PROCEDURES in detail without executing them for real; then, actually running the plan''s systems in parallel alongside normal production operations to confirm they genuinely work; and finally, a full test that actually interrupts normal production operations entirely to confirm the plan works completely for real. What is this progression of BCP/DR testing rigor called?');
VALUES ('e08922b0-f9ce-4ac9-b7d8-61b543ab825d', '0ba48048-fdae-4738-8aa0-3f6056a9f37f', 'This progression directly extends the tabletop exercise concept (covered generally in a prior paper, as the lowest-rigor, discussion-based method) into the FULL spectrum of standard BCP/DR test types, each providing genuinely increasing real-world confidence at correspondingly increasing cost, complexity, and operational risk: a tabletop exercise walks through the plan at a HIGH level as group discussion, with no actual technical execution of anything at all. A walkthrough goes a level deeper, reviewing the SPECIFIC, detailed, step-by-step procedures the plan actually calls for (who does exactly what, in what order) without yet actually executing any of those steps for real. A parallel test moves to genuine technical execution — actually standing up and running the recovery systems ALONGSIDE normal production operations, confirming those systems genuinely work as intended, but without touching or disrupting production at all, since production keeps running normally the whole time. A full interruption test is the most rigorous and realistic of all — actually stopping normal production operations entirely and genuinely relying on the recovery plan to bring things back, providing the strongest possible real-world confidence the plan actually works under genuinely real conditions, but at the real, meaningful cost of actually disrupting live production operations to run the test itself. This directly connects to and extends the hot/warm/cold alternate-site cost-versus-readiness trade-off (covered generally in a prior paper) — a genuinely similar underlying principle of increasing rigor and realism trading off against increasing cost and risk, applied here specifically to how the plan itself is TESTED and validated, not merely designed.');
